#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087460 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "58d92951-d993-50ec-806c-802c627ada96" :question/math-academy-id "e-5213" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $x^2 + 3y^2 - 4x - 6y = 2$ (shown above) intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/850/Source/Images/7a5d03c5fc0581217a488b76e6d39486.png)" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x$. We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 3y^{2} - 4x - 6y &= 2 \\\\\nx^{2} + 3 \\cdot 0^{2} - 4x - 6 \\cdot 0 &= 2 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x &= 2 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 4x + 4) - 4 &= 2 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} - 4 &= 2 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} &= 6 \\\\\nx - 2 &= ± \\sqrt{6} \\\\\nx &= 2 ± \\sqrt{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{6},0)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087461 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "a7fe6697-669c-57e2-8415-4bd40cf76dac" :question/math-academy-id "e-5214" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $(x-2)^2 + \\dfrac{(y-2)^2}{4} = 1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 2)^{2} + ((y - 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} + ((0 - 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} + 1 &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx - 2 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(2,0)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/850/Source/Images/c435c838bf3a88b169499c58834c75c4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087465 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "85e115e3-4f55-53e6-9d3b-decb3dc02267" :question/math-academy-id "e-5220" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4}+\\dfrac{(y-3)^2}{16}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - 1)^{2})/(4) + ((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1 \\\\\n((0 - 1)^{2})/(4) + ((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4} + ((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1 \\\\\n((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\n(y - 3)^{2} &= 12 \\\\\ny - 3 &= ± \\sqrt{12} \\\\\ny - 3 &= 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\\ny &= 3 ± 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,3 -2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,3 +2\\sqrt{3})$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/850/Source/Images/686d5b60b8ea3f16af57af3ed5ef341d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "5cbcffa6-eb3d-5438-ba08-4b47cbbf76ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-5221" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $5x^2 + 2y^2 - 20x - 8y + 13 = 0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y$. We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 20x - 8y + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 0^{2} + 2y^{2} - 20 \\cdot 0 - 8y + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2y^{2} - 8y + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2(y^{2} - 4y) + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2[y^{2} - 4y + 4 - 4] + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2[(y - 2)^{2} - 4] + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2(y - 2)^{2} + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= -\\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n(y-2)^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\n, there is no $y$ such that\n\n$$\n(y-2)^2 = -\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/850/Source/Images/dadec72b5f61bde4c28bdf1079eb4729.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcf660b4-d4f5-5e3c-bbc1-0920eaadb2d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267862} {:answer/id #uuid "7538da33-4499-5a56-8ee1-78740b96168b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267863} {:answer/id #uuid "f715465a-681c-5546-aaf4-b72dcccc54c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267864} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1a01ae-c095-5d05-8219-c89f6c6f83db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267865} {:answer/id #uuid "8d804631-6000-5274-9589-897a4691f407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f715465a-681c-5546-aaf4-b72dcccc54c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267864} :answer-field/id #uuid "06d0653b-a7e0-51d3-b91c-7d5c19f29317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267861}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fca3c3f7-188d-5506-9818-be66c0607803" :question/math-academy-id "q-16642" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{4}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}+16 & =36 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{20}{9}} \\\\ x & =3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(3-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)$ and $(3+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ad77f8-16b1-5bb8-ad29-59b9ef0fc86e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267869} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb48277-c593-5ee0-8b65-8b6011b2eb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267870} {:answer/id #uuid "e56e4c0e-b228-5a52-8826-1f64bc0761ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267871} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b23ee3-7a6f-51f4-98c2-c0dccec79917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267872} {:answer/id #uuid "26a1c010-0e1b-5e5b-bc36-75a8df257575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e56e4c0e-b228-5a52-8826-1f64bc0761ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267871} :answer-field/id #uuid "b32eca97-6dd3-5ee1-a315-2b1fcbdb1bb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267868}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50559ddf-048e-5af7-bb48-9f6c1a546726" :question/math-academy-id "q-16646" :question/problem "Calculate the points if any, where the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+2(y-4)^{2}+20=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2(y-4)^{2} & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2(0-4)^{2} & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2(16) & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+32 & =-20 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{52}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $x$ such that ${x}^{2}=-\\frac{52}{5}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $x$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $x$-axis at any point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6b7ca58-6523-5b4a-89aa-333c3f91d9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267876} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d2ee77-b7e7-5264-8fa6-a323a3d65daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267877} {:answer/id #uuid "e283c48b-6c89-599d-b881-478cdb455ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267878} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb02340-833a-56ca-8205-4cd7a9dbc4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267879} {:answer/id #uuid "d8635ba2-c02d-5b70-adf7-4d27026af194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cb02340-833a-56ca-8205-4cd7a9dbc4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267879} :answer-field/id #uuid "64d96e1d-dc55-5d53-92ab-ea6ad3148922" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267875}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69c2f5cb-5a68-5c5e-91e4-a196dc268883" :question/math-academy-id "q-16647" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2}=72$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}+9\\cdot {0}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2} & =72 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm \\sqrt{18} \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(3-3\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(3+3\\sqrt{2},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f0d9c56-8bc0-59bb-aaaa-ba7b2d977ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 4.5)$ and $(0, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084922} {:answer/id #uuid "b95182a8-65ec-5089-832d-a4feab67c09a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 4.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084923} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9d5abd-983f-55c1-80f1-68f0a0e45f75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 1.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084924} {:answer/id #uuid "5325a49f-8386-539a-9551-50271273c3e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 1.5)$ and $(0, 4.5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084925} {:answer/id #uuid "6b504e5c-db51-5203-aabf-1e5b16a33fb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b504e5c-db51-5203-aabf-1e5b16a33fb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084926} :answer-field/id #uuid "3be5e4f8-3f21-5664-af45-90a5f44162d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084921}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93774ab1-c034-5d25-91bb-1dbd3d1f509d" :question/math-academy-id "q-16682" :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse $((x - 3)^{2})/(4) + ((y - 2)^{2})/(9) = 1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}\\ge 0$, there is no $y$ such that $\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=-\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/assets/e2d4eadef6206fee8006028477c30337e2019000c33ed971f1f048294ef72615.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad101bbd-2947-5f49-aece-d36f4d805851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267883} {:answer/id #uuid "c4eb3833-664e-5674-95bb-5ad675bd5959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm 4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267884} {:answer/id #uuid "3f01f6b0-8922-5a07-9fde-22aa4b0105b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(7,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267885} {:answer/id #uuid "0221d733-e017-502e-a01a-2b1025b7e60f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267886} {:answer/id #uuid "fc414fcd-71aa-5c5a-868f-9550b1919c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0)$ and $(3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f01f6b0-8922-5a07-9fde-22aa4b0105b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(7,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267885} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b3ca1a7-a59a-52f0-8e90-2a7cb91cb05e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267882}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "826b5f6d-dee6-5776-af58-0c5d7c0d4784" :question/math-academy-id "q-170548" :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-6x+12y=7$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-6x+12y & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3(0)^{2}-6x+12(0) & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x & =7 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x+9)-9 & =7 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-1,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(-1,0)$ and $(7,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb381392-dd0c-5031-a6d4-51dd84f7d05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267890} {:answer/id #uuid "1c3b44c9-e455-58b0-b785-923ebcea6e05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb381392-dd0c-5031-a6d4-51dd84f7d05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267890} :answer-field/id #uuid "562a30ea-afbe-5415-b15a-a7db1322040a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267889} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d37a08f-69d9-5078-acd5-933b069748f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d37a08f-69d9-5078-acd5-933b069748f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267893} :answer-field/id #uuid "12bbe58d-42cb-5971-ac34-b3f67cde70e4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267892} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbc66a3c-e17c-5dc3-8e7a-24d4f5327de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbc66a3c-e17c-5dc3-8e7a-24d4f5327de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267895} :answer-field/id #uuid "252f5f8a-0757-5141-bc1d-baca801b8376" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267894} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7698812-d39d-5e8b-a8a7-2d56f3b2fd6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7698812-d39d-5e8b-a8a7-2d56f3b2fd6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267897} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ff994ce-6c68-5b05-9487-1719f2ac08dd" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267896}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9aa6c2dc-573d-5d58-b8d8-b009d90b1d9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-274483" :question/problem "From **left to right**, the ellipse $25{x}^{2}+14{y}^{2}-50x-11y=29$ intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}+14{y}^{2}-50x-11y & =29 \\\\ 25{x}^{2}+14(0)^{2}-50x-11(0) & =29 \\\\ 25{x}^{2}-50x & =29 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =\\frac{29}{25} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2} & =\\frac{29}{25} \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{29}{25}+1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{29}{25}+\\frac{25}{25} \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{54}{25} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{5} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(1-\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{5},0)\\,\\text{and}\\,B(1+\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{5},0).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "962dd733-4b99-5f4e-a2d6-7748e34715c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "962dd733-4b99-5f4e-a2d6-7748e34715c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267900} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d01f169-3f21-50a1-9925-faa3b203818f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267899} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fd066ff-756a-5c80-b604-3622a23bdc28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fd066ff-756a-5c80-b604-3622a23bdc28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267902} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bfc2aae-ea10-5a5f-a4ba-439e30119335" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267901} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a080165c-efbb-5271-af0f-1278c6c2a7d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a080165c-efbb-5271-af0f-1278c6c2a7d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267904} :answer-field/id #uuid "71c11fd5-f6f5-52a7-bf86-f55919992d76" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267903}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2f660de-a80f-5e8f-b868-1fdd7f337e63" :question/math-academy-id "q-274493" :question/problem "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{45}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,1+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{45}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+5)^{2}}{45}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{9}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ 10+9(y-1)^{2} & =18 \\\\ 9(y-1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{9} \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ y & =1\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,1+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,1-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "030c1d50-b0c4-540b-b069-c27f8d8ba509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "030c1d50-b0c4-540b-b069-c27f8d8ba509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267907} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d3a3e0-64e0-5c6f-9933-31ed75050903" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267906} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fb58b5d-2122-500e-a810-9489dfb9887f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fb58b5d-2122-500e-a810-9489dfb9887f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267909} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd7af845-2aff-51a0-a3f1-a1945da83997" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267908} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a34f9223-ea06-5e25-b5d3-1267da5adaed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a34f9223-ea06-5e25-b5d3-1267da5adaed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267911} :answer-field/id #uuid "83386bc0-5376-5dd6-8a06-92d4f8f1efe5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267910} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1986cfaf-bd94-50b0-88d8-f1e32fed741f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1986cfaf-bd94-50b0-88d8-f1e32fed741f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267913} :answer-field/id #uuid "19dc6219-b2dd-5439-877c-a94b4345925f" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267912}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf58ef6b-adb6-50de-bf09-a945b0f3c527" :question/math-academy-id "q-274502" :question/problem "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-10x-32y+54=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-10x-32y+54 & =0 \\\\ 2(0)^{2}+4{y}^{2}-10\\cdot 0-32y+54 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-32y+54 & =0 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-8y]+54 & =0 \\\\ 4[(y-4)^{2}-16]+54 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-4)^{2}-64+54 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-4)^{2}-10 & =0 \\\\ 4(y-4)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =\\frac{10}{4} \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,4+\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,4-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa95007f-6fd9-5756-801b-816fb1df33c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa95007f-6fd9-5756-801b-816fb1df33c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267916} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4458648-e014-54c8-815f-21e40d8b9198" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267915} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "930ecd69-e12f-50bf-b093-e7aeb4035088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "930ecd69-e12f-50bf-b093-e7aeb4035088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267918} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9b2c65e-e8f1-5ef1-83b8-6dfe7c5bedce" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267917}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df2710fa-49d8-5662-a9c3-1c15d9f9c3a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-325860" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{36}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\nThe $x$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(0-6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{36}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9} & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(-3,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/assets/5a1377fa0ff46ff7a7e4b685d38852ad67abd8c0adfc432fc22d7c1d57b7a57f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb4755f7-1c48-50c6-b029-095b15fea548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb4755f7-1c48-50c6-b029-095b15fea548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267921} :answer-field/id #uuid "4542b178-f501-5ff4-9a1b-6bc1493eb5ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267920} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f153456-9f55-5a0c-b65b-e4d7863b0237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f153456-9f55-5a0c-b65b-e4d7863b0237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267923} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e660fa3-1d44-5dd8-85e8-417da42ca5e4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267922}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdd1251d-a8ec-542d-984d-1d545114b93f" :question/math-academy-id "q-326043" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse  $9{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}+18x+32y+9=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\nThe $x$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}+18x+32y+9 & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}+8\\cdot {0}^{2}+18x+32\\cdot 0+9 & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}+18x+9 & =0 \\\\ 9\\,[{x}^{2}+2x]+9 & =0 \\\\ 9[(x+1)^{2}-1]+9 & =0 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}-9+9 & =0 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at  $(-1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb300948-7c44-5978-8673-8e6445dbdd69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb300948-7c44-5978-8673-8e6445dbdd69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267926} :answer-field/id #uuid "5763ef00-c15b-522c-aca2-e17c909edca9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267925} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d50caa28-cdf3-52d0-b59d-799c36b1cfd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d50caa28-cdf3-52d0-b59d-799c36b1cfd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267928} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d04626d-f77e-590f-9a73-c86657f38796" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267927} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69f0d2b8-3113-5479-96e0-e15c830241d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69f0d2b8-3113-5479-96e0-e15c830241d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267930} :answer-field/id #uuid "070fb05e-c8a0-52ed-88e4-ace1bbf1403c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267929}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0c5d322-3f98-5330-8cc0-62528a79d8ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-326289" :question/problem "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,2+\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0+3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{12}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & =1 \\\\ 108\\,(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}) & =108 \\\\ 81+4(y-2)^{2} & =108 \\\\ 4(y-2)^{2} & =27 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{27}{4} \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =2\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,2+\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,2-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce037c27-1191-59ab-91c7-ce7d84ebb5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce037c27-1191-59ab-91c7-ce7d84ebb5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267933} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e89b1e-0fa0-5146-819c-88d513ab13c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b790113-deef-5aab-be8b-e1d9d281a34d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b790113-deef-5aab-be8b-e1d9d281a34d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267935} :answer-field/id #uuid "782fc26c-27bb-5025-9bdd-006f8586f5cb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267934}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "167fbd42-673a-5411-ba0f-804405134964" :question/math-academy-id "q-327146" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-5y+\\frac{25}{4}=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8(0)-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/assets/0a1303644a54b07f874df4f6694d84384e634403bc4efe8ee5086d922bc6f25e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a5bbf6c-e006-5462-96c2-01a04a7d3215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267938} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea08dd6-6a69-50f6-a4c6-a49715cace38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ea08dd6-6a69-50f6-a4c6-a49715cace38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267939} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b420753-4fe9-5574-8fbd-9c1fec598750" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267937} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7903531c-ae30-505e-a66f-7a39f8247464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7903531c-ae30-505e-a66f-7a39f8247464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267941} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b796882-9ebf-5199-84e3-4840db2fe99e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267940}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c3360fd-5dbf-5830-b141-90394d4fbf4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-327148" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x+y+\\frac{1}{4}=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x+y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ 3(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-12(0)+y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+\\frac{1}{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-\\frac{1}{2}\\,).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/assets/94a5bd2df6ae6177e62b66a0a978a358d73fed255e425337812887c81b9d2d10.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e85a367-2302-57e9-962b-f328530e6515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267944} {:answer/id #uuid "48c2c761-77e4-540e-b74a-7c7343e36a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267945} {:answer/id #uuid "4c01566b-b810-5053-86db-734c63ece004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267946} {:answer/id #uuid "e967b97f-698b-5bc0-9412-c86232ffb994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267947} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa028fc-f755-56af-8dd4-05d189f371f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e85a367-2302-57e9-962b-f328530e6515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267944} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c7a00b5-b64e-53d2-b2a0-a158b06a42ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267943}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64b3860f-fd90-5411-a2e5-411bcddf0dbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72914" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y+1=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-4x-2(0)+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{3},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the y-Intercepts of an 